  "prediction": "The Public Safety Officers' Benefits (PSOB) and Educational Assistance (PSOEA) programs provide cash benefits to families of federal, state, and local law enforcement officers, firefighters, emergency management agency employees, and emergency medical services members who are killed or permanently disabled in the line of duty. The programs are administered by the PSOB Office of the Department of Justice (DOJ), Bureau of Justice Assistance (BJA), and funded through annual appropriations. Eligibility for PSOB benefits includes law enforcement officers, firefighters, chaplains, FEMA employees, state or local emergency management agency employees, and emergency medical services members. To be eligible for PSOB death or disability benefits, a person must have served in a public agency, which includes federal, state, or local governments, and must have sustained a personal injury in the line of duty. The PSOB program covers injuries caused by external forces, chemicals, electricity, climatic conditions, infectious diseases, radiation, virii, or bacteria. Death benefits are payable to the spouse and children of the public safety officer, while disability benefits are paid only in cases of permanent and total disability. The PSOEA program provides higher-education assistance to the children and spouses of public safety officers killed or permanently disabled in the line of duty. The monthly full-time attendance PSOEA assistance is $1,224, and the benefit amounts are indexed to reflect changes in the cost of living. Both programs have specific eligibility requirements, application deadlines, and benefit payment procedures.",
  "scored_prediction": "The Public Safety Officers' Benefits (PSOB) and Educational Assistance (PSOEA) programs provide cash benefits to families of federal, state, and local law enforcement officers, firefighters, emergency management agency employees, and emergency medical services members who are killed or permanently disabled in the line of duty. The programs are administered by the PSOB Office of the Department of Justice (DOJ), Bureau of Justice Assistance (BJA), and funded through annual appropriations. Eligibility for PSOB benefits includes law enforcement officers, firefighters, chaplains, FEMA employees, state or local emergency management agency employees, and emergency medical services members. To be eligible for PSOB death or disability benefits, a person must have served in a public agency, which includes federal, state, or local governments, and must have sustained a personal injury in the line of duty. The PSOB program covers injuries caused by external forces, chemicals, electricity, climatic conditions, infectious diseases, radiation, virii, or bacteria. Death benefits are payable to the spouse and children of the public safety officer, while disability benefits are paid only in cases of permanent and total disability. The PSOEA program provides higher-education assistance to the children and spouses of public safety officers killed or permanently disabled in the line of duty. The monthly full-time attendance PSOEA assistance is $1,224, and the benefit amounts are indexed to reflect changes in the cost of living. Both programs have specific eligibility requirements, application deadlines, and benefit payment procedures.",
  "score": 49.02723685427486,
  "answers": [
    "The Public Safety Officers' Benefits (PSOB) program provides cash benefits to federal, state, and local law enforcement officers; firefighters; employees of emergency management agencies; and members of emergency medical services agencies who are killed or permanently and totally disabled as the result of personal injuries sustained in the line of duty. The Public Safety Officers' Educational Assistance (PSOEA) program, a component of the PSOB program, provides higher-education assistance to the children and spouses of public safety officers killed or permanently disabled in the line of duty. The PSOB and PSOEA programs are administered by the Department of Justice (DOJ), Bureau of Justice Assistance (BJA). However, claimants dissatisfied with denials of benefits may pursue administrative appeals within DOJ and may seek judicial review before the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit. Each year, Congress appropriates funding for PSOB death benefits, which is considered mandatory spending, and for PSOB disability benefits and PSOEA benefits, which is subject to annual appropriations. For FY2019, the one-time lump-sum PSOB death and disability benefit is $359,316 and the PSOEA monthly benefit for a student attending an educational institution full-time is $1,224. In FY2017, the DOJ approved 399 claims for PSOB death benefits, 82 claims for PSOB disability benefits, and 601 claims for PSOEA benefits."
  ],
